<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Songkhla</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Singora</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Songkhla is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, on a narrow strip of land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a> and the Songkhla Lake lagoon. Early in the 17th century a Muslim ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dato-mogol">Dato Mogol</a>, founded a fortified port at Singora on the hills nearby, which traded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> destroyed it in 1680. The present city was laid out on the eastern shore in the 19th century and is the capital of Songkhla province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/songkhla.jpg" alt="Songkhla" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tarik Abdel Monem · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_of_Songkhla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Songkhla lies at the opening of Songkhla Lake to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> near the border with Malaysia, 968 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>. It is a fishing town and an important harbour, the major seaport on the east side of the Isthmus of Kra. It is the capital of Songkhla Province, though smaller than the neighbouring Hat Yai, and with it forms the Greater Hat Yai-Songkhla Metropolitan Area. The climate is tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, with a short dry season in February and March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name means the city of lions and is the Thai form of Singgora, referring to a lion-shaped mountain near the city. Excavations on the isthmus between the lake and the sea show that from the tenth to the fourteenth century it was a major urbanised area and a centre of maritime trade, especially with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The state that existed there had the short <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> name Singhapura, and the vernacular Satingpra. Its ruins lie a few kilometres north of Songkhla; Satingpra was among the most important trading centres of the Tambralinga Kingdom, with a brick citadel inside a moat and a system of canals joining the sea to the lake.</p><p>The precursor of the present town was the Sultanate of Singora, a heavily fortified port founded in the early seventeenth century by a Persian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dato-mogol">Dato Mogol</a>, and flourishing under his son Sultan Sulaiman Shah. After decades of conflict it was destroyed and abandoned in 1680. Its forts, city walls, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> cemetery and the tomb of Sultan Sulaiman Shah remain. On 8 December 1941 the Imperial Japanese army landed at Songkhla, invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and beginning its advance south in the Malayan campaign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The population was 61,758 in 2020. Most residents are Buddhist, with a large proportion of Muslims, especially in the rural areas near the Malaysian border, who speak Yawi, a language closely related to Malay.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Songkhla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The account calls it a branch of Funan and places it on the Malay Peninsula, probably in the region of Kelantan, Pattani or <b>Songkhla</b>, but its location has never been confirmed.</blockquote><a href="/state/chi-tu">Chi Tu</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1605 Dato Mogol, a Muslim leader said to be of Persian or Malay origin, established a fortified port at <b>Singora</b>, present-day Songkhla, on the east coast of the peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-singora">Sultanate of Singora</a></li></ul></section>
